+# ECON-03c1 — Macro NPC consumption and shortage horizon
+
+## Decision
+
+Population needs are settled **immediately at a scheduled consumption boundary**,
+not continuously and not by running one cooldown timer per NPC. A 20-tick needs
+period is the current compatibility default; it is a *game-time abstraction*,
+not a claim that humans eat or breathe every 20 real-world seconds.
+
+Each active physical supply compartment has an aggregate consumer count and
+amounts of food, water, oxygen, wastewater, organic waste, and CO2. At a due
+boundary, a single deterministic batch deducts actual resources up to available
+stock and credits equal mass to the corresponding waste streams. Supplies
+that are missing stay missing: no fabricated food, water, oxygen, or waste.
+The shortage changes viability/comfort and can generate supply demand.
+
+## Scale and cooldown
+
+- A cohort is a count of residents sharing materially relevant attributes, NOT
+  one fake NPC whose personality stands in for everyone.
+- Consumption cost is proportional to cohort size, but computation is
+  proportional to **number of material compartments/cohorts**, not headcount.
+- A due-window count for an unchanged time span is
+  `floor(target_tick / cooldown) - floor(previous_tick / cooldown)`. It can be
+  computed in constant time even after a very long offline interval.
+- The prototype's ration is 1 game-kg each of food, water and oxygen per NPC
+  per 20 ticks, preserving the earlier game balancing. Rations and distinct
+  food/water/air cadences can become policy/biome dependent later.
+- Time-skipping *only consumption* is valid while population, production,
+  stock transfers, waste processing, utilities and policies remain unchanged.
+  A full cold-region skip must process other scheduled events in causal order
+  or use an exactly equivalent aggregate solver. Do not jump ahead through
+  unprocessed production or shipments.
+- The scheduler avoids integer multiplication overflow. Supplies and waste
+  bins use signed 64-bit kg counters. Thus, trillions of simulated residents
+  are feasible per cohort, but galaxy-wide simulation is not yet proved
+  scalable end-to-end: locality, active-agent promotion, memory and persistent
+  storage still require separate scale tests.
+
+## Runway and market integration
+
+A `ResourceMarket::SiteView` now reports each staffed farm and occupied habitat
+**separately**, with its physically present supplies:
+
+- next consumption boundary tick
+- whole rations available for food, water and oxygen
+- first boundary when at least one requirement cannot be fully satisfied
+
+This is a no-production/no-resupply estimate, **not a prediction that nobody
+will produce goods or repair the situation**. Site-level market summaries may
+also count stored warehouse goods, but do not pretend that they have already
+been physically delivered or plumbed to a habitat.
+
+`RESOURCE_RUNWAY` telemetry carries one record per service compartment every
+20 ticks alongside the existing read-only `RESOURCE_MARKET` feed.
+
+## Non-goals and next gates
+
+- No per-NPC timer or trillions of physical NPC objects are allocated.
+- No real-time physiology, population growth, individual deaths, or involuntary
+  full-auto offline colony growth is introduced.
+- No wages, monetary economy or player trading are reinstated.
+- The current native Simulation still steps its active world once per tick;
+  cold-region event queues and catch-up for all production/transport workflows
+  are a **separate performance milestone**.
+- Existing explicit residents, NPC relationships and job assignments remain
+  important near the player; only macro needs should be aggregated.
+- Next: use forecasted shortage deadlines to prioritize authorized physical
+  shipments, then implement trustworthy reservation/hauling receipts.
